    <fr:title text="Power series">Power series</fr:title>
  </fr:frontmatter>
  <fr:mainmatter>
    <html:p>Power series are important, thus the spoiler: every function that's analytic in some neighborhood has a power series representation, i.e. it can be written as a power series (in that neighborhood).</html:p>
    <fr:tree show-metadata="false">
      <fr:frontmatter>
        <fr:authors />
        <fr:taxon>Theorem</fr:taxon>
      </fr:frontmatter>
      <fr:mainmatter>
        <html:p>Let <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\sum _{n}^{\infty } f_n]]></fr:tex> be a series of continuous functions on an open set <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[D\subseteq  \mathbb {C}]]></fr:tex> that converges uniformly to <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[f]]></fr:tex> on <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[D]]></fr:tex>. Then <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[f]]></fr:tex> is continuous on <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[D]]></fr:tex>.</html:p>
      </fr:mainmatter>
    </fr:tree>
    <fr:tree show-metadata="false">
      <fr:frontmatter>
        <fr:authors />
        <fr:title text="Power series">Power series</fr:title>
        <fr:taxon>Definition</fr:taxon>
      </fr:frontmatter>
      <fr:mainmatter>
        <html:p>A <html:em>power series</html:em> is a series of the form <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[\sum _{n=0}^{\infty } a_n (z-z_0)^n \ ,]]></fr:tex> where <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[a_n]]></fr:tex> is a sequence in <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[C]]></fr:tex> and <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[z_0 \in  \mathbb {C}]]></fr:tex> is an arbitrary point.</html:p>
        <html:p>By setting <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[w = z-z_0]]></fr:tex>, we can write the series like <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[\sum _{n=0}^{\infty } a_n w^n \ ,]]></fr:tex> and we often use this shortcut to talk about power series, by assuming wlog. that <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[z_0 = 0]]></fr:tex>.</html:p>
      </fr:mainmatter>
    </fr:tree>
    <fr:tree show-metadata="false">
      <fr:frontmatter>
        <fr:authors />
        <fr:title text="Power series convergence criteria">Power series convergence criteria</fr:title>
        <fr:taxon>Theorem</fr:taxon>
      </fr:frontmatter>
      <fr:mainmatter>
        <html:p>
        Let <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[f]]></fr:tex> be a power series as in the above definition and let <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[L := \limsup _{n \to  \infty } \sqrt [n]{|a_n|}]]></fr:tex>.
        <html:ol><html:li>If <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[L = 0]]></fr:tex>, then the power series <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[f]]></fr:tex> converges for all <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[z \in  \mathbb {C}]]></fr:tex>.</html:li>
            <html:li>If <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[L = \infty ]]></fr:tex>, then the power series <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[f]]></fr:tex> converges only for <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[z = z_0]]></fr:tex>.</html:li>
            <html:li>If <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[L \in  (0,\infty )]]></fr:tex>, then the power series <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[f]]></fr:tex> converges for <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[|z-z_0|<R]]></fr:tex>, and diverges for <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[|z-z_0| > R]]></fr:tex>, where <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[R = 1/L]]></fr:tex>.</html:li></html:ol></html:p>
        <html:p />
      </fr:mainmatter>
    </fr:tree>
    <fr:tree show-metadata="false">
